{% block project_guidance %}
You are working on the tests for `mngr forward` (`libs/mngr_forward/`), the
proxy that serves each agent at its own `agent-<hex>.localhost` origin behind
a one-time-code session. Its behaviors are observable at the HTTP and
WebSocket surface, so witness them in process wherever that surface reaches:

- Default to `starlette.testclient.TestClient` against the FastAPI app, the
  idiom `imbue/mngr_forward/server_test.py` already uses. Where a path needs
  real network I/O, that file stands up a backend on loopback (real HTTP and
  real `ws_serve` WebSocket servers) rather than reaching outside the test
  process -- follow that.
- Colocate each witness next to the module whose surface it exercises
  (`server_test.py`, `auth_test.py`, `cookie_test.py`, and so on), matching
  the surrounding tests' fixtures and idioms.
- Read `libs/mngr_forward/conftest.py` and
  `imbue/mngr_forward/testing.py` before writing new setup, and reuse what is
  there. Never use unittest.mock or monkeypatch.setattr.

## What this run is

Every unit in this corpus is currently unwitnessed: the coverage matrix
reports `none` for all of them. You are in the create regime, not the polish
regime. Expect that most of your task is writing new tests, and that an empty
changes object for a unit is almost certainly wrong here, rather than the good
outcome it would be on an already-witnessed corpus.

## Existing tests are the first place to look

`libs/mngr_forward/` already has substantial tests that carry no `witnesses`
markers, and some already verify, in whole or in part, units in your task.
Before writing any new test, search the existing tests for one that exercises
your unit's surface, and prefer marking and extending it over writing a
parallel test beside it. Duplicating coverage that already exists is a defect
in this run, not neutral. Reshaping a test you have adopted so that it
verifies its unit's scope exactly is recorded under IMPROVE_TEST.

## Finish every unit, and be exact about what "finished" means

This run is budgeted for completeness. The deliverable is a suite that is
still trusted in a year: it fails when specified behavior breaks, and does not
fail otherwise. Every ruling below follows from that.

Two checks decide any assertion you are considering. Apply them literally:

- Name the clause. Before writing an assertion, name the step or in-scope Rule
  clause it witnesses -- as written in the file, not scope you infer the
  author intended. If you cannot name one, do not write it: the Trace check
  obligates you to delete it later, so writing it only burns budget. "Being
  thorough" never justifies an assertion; only a clause does.
- Match the clause's precision. Assert exactly what the clause claims. Where a
  step names HTTP 403, assert 403. Where it says "authentication is refused,
  and no session is established", assert the refusal and the absent session,
  and do not pin details the step leaves open (exact status, page wording,
  header order): a correct implementation change can flip those while every
  step stays true, and an assertion that fires on such a change trains
  maintainers to ignore the suite.

Run the same test forward to define complete: every Then/And clause of the
unit needs a witnessing assertion that fails if that clause stops holding. A
clause with none is your gap. Close gaps by building reach, not by widening
assertions elsewhere. Missing setup is something you build in this run, not a
reason to stop.

Point the verdict pressure the right way. What decides a unit's reachable
verdict is whether its claim is closed-world or open-world:

- Closed-world: the claim ranges over a domain you can enumerate -- a finite
  set of paths, statuses, cookie states, origins. Every Scenario and Scenario
  Outline is closed-world, and so are many Rules: a Rule naming four ways a
  cookie is invalid is four checks. Drive these to FULL. It is reachable, so
  it is expected.
- Open-world: the claim quantifies over an unbounded domain -- "under any
  interleaving of requests", "over its whole lifetime", "every URL the proxy
  hands out", any property over all future backends. No finite set of tests
  closes it, so FULL is not merely hard here, it is unavailable, and you
  cannot do otherwise. Witness it by attacking the property, then write a
  partial= note naming the exact quantifier no test can cross. That verdict is
  PARTIAL_STEADY and it is this unit's completion -- do not churn past it.

Never clear a truthful partial= note to claim FULL. A false FULL hides an
unprotected gap from every future maintainer; it is the worst outcome this run
can produce, worse than any honest partial.

PARTIAL_IMPROVABLE and NONE are the failure verdicts. Before reporting either,
reread your own partial= note: if it names effort ("would need a fixture",
"would need a live backend"), the residue is improvable and building that
setup is your job now, on this budget. Only an open-world quantifier earns
STEADY.
